    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d85">

                <pb facs="0106" n="92" xml:id="pg92"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/3929: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-01T18:00:00+07:00">June 1, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 1—2:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>956. Director [of] Foreign Office’s European Department<anchor n="87"
                        xml:id="d85-f1"/> last evening said privately to Counselor that he did not
                    believe “rumors” that Japan and Soviet Russia had come to agreement and did not
                    believe more than two divisions Jap troops had been moved from Manchuria into
                    China proper. He said Japs could move some troops out of Manchuria without much
                    risk because Soviets had probably transferred some Siberian forces to European
                    theatre. He considered present military situation Honan and Yangtze Valley
                    serious, with possibility Changsha would be lost before long.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d85-f1" n="87" xml:id="d85-f1-content">
